      },
      "text": "## The Big Picture: Recovering Depth from Two Eyes"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md#002",
      "path": [
        "06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:computer-vision:06-multiview-geometry, git-history:computer-vision:06-multiview-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "computer-vision/06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md",
        "concepts": "[epipolar geometry, fundamental matrix, essential matrix, stereo, triangulation, RANSAC, homography]",
        "current_path": "computer-vision/06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:computer-vision:multiview-geometry",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "computer-vision",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[multi-view geometry]",
        "section": "computing-software",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Multi-View Geometry",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "A single image destroys depth (guide 01: projection divides by `Z`). Two images of the\nsame scene from different viewpoints recover it — the difference in where a point appears\n(its *disparity*) encodes its distance. Multi-view geometry is the precise machinery for\nthis: the **epipolar constraint** that links the two views, the **fundamental** and\n**essential** matrices that encode it, **triangulation** that reconstructs 3D points, and\n**RANSAC** that makes it all robust to wrong matches. This is the geometric heart of\nvision — the part deep learning has *not* replaced."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md#003",
      "path": [
        "06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:computer-vision:06-multiview-geometry, git-history:computer-vision:06-multiview-geometry]",
        "canonical_path": "computer-vision/06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md",
        "concepts": "[epipolar geometry, fundamental matrix, essential matrix, stereo, triangulation, RANSAC, homography]",
        "current_path": "computer-vision/06-MULTIVIEW-GEOMETRY.md",
        "id": "maxim:computer-vision:multiview-geometry",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "computer-vision",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[multi-view geometry]",
        "section": "computing-software",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Multi-View Geometry",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|              TWO VIEWS RECOVER THE THIRD DIMENSION                           |\n|                                                                              |\n|                          X  (3D point, unknown depth)                        |\n|                         /|\\                                                  |\n|                        / | \\                                                 |\n|                       /  |  \\                                                |\n|                      /   |   \\                                               |\n|        image 1 -----x1   |   x2----- image 2                                 |\n|                   /      |      \\                                            |\n|                  C1------|-------C2     two camera centers                   |\n|                  (left)  |   (right)                                         |\n|                          v                                                   |\n|                     baseline C1-C2 + the two rays -> triangulate X           |\n|                                                                              |\n|   Disparity (x1 - x2) is INVERSELY proportional to depth Z.                  |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
